<header><p class="st-kicker">city state · Anatolia and the Levant</p><h1 class="st-title">Beirut</h1><p class="st-meta">1500 BCE – 1150 BCE (approx.) · capital Beirut</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">Beirut, a Late Bronze Age Canaanite port known from the Amarna letters, ancestor of the modern Lebanese capital.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>Biruta grew on a promontory on the central Lebanese coast, settled since prehistory. By the 15th century BCE it was a small walled port town under the overlordship of the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/egyptian-empire-new-kingdom">Egyptian Empire New Kingdom</a>.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>Its ruler <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ammunira">Ammunira</a> wrote to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pharaoh">pharaoh</a> in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/amarna-letters">Amarna letters</a>, promising loyalty and sending reports. When <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rib-hadda">Rib-Hadda</a> of <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/byblos">Byblos</a> was driven out of his city by a rebellion, he took refuge in Beirut. Excavations in the city centre after the civil war uncovered its <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bronze-age">Bronze Age</a> walls.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>The Canaanite town shared the decline of the coast in the 12th century BCE, and in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iron-age">Iron Age</a> remained in the shadow of <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/sidon">Sidon</a> and <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/tyre">Tyre</a>. It later grew into the Roman colony of Berytus.</p><p class="st-conf">Few sources survive for this state; details are uncertain.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/beirut.jpg" alt="Beirut" width="320" height="195"><figcaption>Image: Richardsaad75 · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Platinum_Tower_-_Beirut_by_Nabil_Gholam_Architects.tif" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1500 BCE – 1150 BCE (approx.)</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/16th-century-bce">16th century BCE</a> – <a href="/centuries/12th-century-bce">12th century BCE</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>city state</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>mercantile</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Beirut</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Ammunira</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Canaanite religion</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Canaanite, Akkadian</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/lebanon">Lebanon</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Biruta, Berytus</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=-1325&amp;focus=beirut" aria-label="Beirut on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5585 949 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Beirut lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M5905 1205L5863 1489"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M5863 1489h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5905 1205h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5905 1205h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks its recorded location. <a href="/atlas?year=-1325&amp;focus=beirut">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>

